e them a reason to come back  and attack those forces and go massively  into ukraine that's one way to get this  started there might be other ways as  well if you remember in 2014  russia used  regular military and special forces in  unmarked uniforms to take over  government buildings and declare an  insurrection and then when ukraine tried  to put down the insurrection massively  slowed in so-called humanitarian  assistance which turned out to be a lot  of weaponry and a lot of troops  but i think that sort of thing softening  up the target creating the pretext it's  also why he wanted to have these  meetings with the us and nato to get a  rejection of his ideas he's trying to  create the pretext for  justifying uh  going into ukraine again so that's how  you read the talks in vienna this week  which seem  frankly forgot nowhere  yes and i think entirely predictable  putin knew that the demands that he put  into the texts that he gave to the us  and nato in december would be absolutely  unacceptable uh nato is not going to say  no countries can ever join again uh nato  is not going to say that we will  withdraw  our military protection of the baltic  states and poland  nato is not going to say that russia has  a right to determine the  future and security aspirations of its  neighbors  knowing that why would he then propose  these things  i think on the one hand of course if we  were to give him that he'd be happy  but more realistically he knew he would  get a rejection and he wanted to show  that he made the effort at dialogue and  diplomacy but he was rejected so  he quote unquote had no choice  there's been a lot of activity this week  um  ukraine was hit by a massive cyber  attack warning its citizens to be  quite afraid and expect the worst now um  we don't know who exactly is behind that  attack but if it does turn out to be  a russian or russian inspired um because  you've seen something similar in the  past um  what action could be taken  well first off let's analyze that attack  a little bit it um  there have been cyber attacks against  ukraine against estonia uh in the united  states against our election system in  germany even in the uk i believe so  it would be fitting the pattern for this  to be russia and surprising if it's not  second what is the message here it is  psychological pressure on ukraine we can  attack your government ministries  anytime we want to we can put up our  messages on those ministries and we only  did the civilian ones this time we  didn't do your military the implicit  threat being we can do your military so  i think this is a lot of psychological  pressure on ukraine as far as what can  be done about cyber attacks it's it's  probably too late to prevent things now  it is something that all of us need to  do in in the west in ukraine in our  partner countries help them build  resilience against cyber attacks uh but  it's something that needs to be done  constantly and with vigilance not  something that at the spur of the moment  we can change mr putin has masked a  hundred thousand russian troops on the  border he says  i can do what i like on my own  soil  but uh it's evident that this is at some  level  um a kind of intimidation what do you  think is larger game is here what is  what is the point of all of this  well i think  it is what he said it is  which is uh he wants to prevent ukraine  from ever being admitted to nato he  wants nato to basically agree to tear up  the helsinki accords and this idea that  every state has a right to its  sovereignty and territorial integrity  every state can choose its own alliances  this foundation of security in europe  that's been in place for 50 years he  wants to tear that up and replace it  with something more like yalta which is  there's an eastern sphere of influence  and that's ours and there's a western  sphere of influence you can play there  and this is a deal between stalin and uh  western leaders correct yes he's looking  to go back 